1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,394
A petrol engine turns only around a third of its fuel into movement. Most of the rest becomes heat.

2
00:00:06,744 --> 00:00:10,540
Coolant flows through passages around the cylinders, soaking up heat.

3
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:15,883
A water pump, driven by the engine or an electric motor, keeps it circulating.

4
00:00:16,233 --> 00:00:24,137
Hot coolant flows through the radiator, where air rushing through thin fins carries the heat away. A fan helps when you are stopped.

5
00:00:24,487 --> 00:00:31,381
When the engine is cold, the thermostat stays shut so it warms up fast. At around ninety degrees, it opens.

6
00:00:31,731 --> 00:00:38,975
Overheating can warp the cylinder head and blow the head gasket. If the temperature gauge climbs high, pull over safely.

7
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:45,893
The system is under pressure. Opening it hot can spray scalding coolant. Always wait for it to cool down.

8
00:00:46,244 --> 00:00:50,668
Pump it round, cool it in the radiator, and let the thermostat decide when.
